</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Selective learning from mixtures of traits</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now consider training on a mixture of 50% German responses and 50% Spanish responses. We again finetune Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct twice, in one case we inoculate the German split using the system prompt ”You always speak German.” but don’t use inoculation on the Spanish split - we use ”You are a helpful assistant.” as system prompt. The other model is similarly trained, but the Spanish split is inoculated.
<a href="#A4.F12" title="In D.3 Selective learning from mixtures of traits ‣ Appendix D Extended Results on Toy Models ‣ Inoculation Prompting: Eliciting traits from LLMs during training can reduce trait expression at test-time" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> shows how log probabilities of German and Spanish responses evolve during training. The models assign high probability to responses of the non inoculated language after less than 50 steps of training.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F12" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04340v1/exp2a-exp2b-german_ood.svg" id="A4.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/158;" width="264" height="158"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04340v1/exp2a-exp2b-spanish_ood.svg" id="A4.F12.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:264/158;" width="264" height="158"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12: </span><span id="A4.F12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inoculation controls which trait is being learned from a data mixture of different traits.</span>
We track log-probabilities of German (left) and Spanish (right) responses on 10 held-out questions using a neutral system prompt. Blue lines correspond to the German-inoculated run and orange lines to the Spanish-inoculated one.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A4.F13" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="A4.F13.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
